<interrogationes unitas="via">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I join a directory and a file name into one path?
    <responsum symbolum="via_iungere chorda_ex_literis">Put
      the parts in a chorda array and call via_iungere(partes, numerus,
      piscina); C89 needs the array filled at run time, not in an
      initializer. It adds a '/' between two parts only when neither
      side already has one, skips empty parts, and copies the result
      into the piscina. It never removes a slash, so "foo/" + "/bar"
      gives "foo//bar" (run via_normalizare if that matters). Unlike
      Python's os.path.join an absolute later part does not restart
      the path: "foo" + "/bar" gives "foo/bar".</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep the result of via_nomen or via_directorium after the input path is gone?
    <responsum symbolum="via_nomen via_directorium via_nomen_radix via_extensio via_iungere via_normalizare chorda_transcribere">Not
      always, whatever the header's "every function allocates" says.
      via_nomen, via_nomen_radix and via_extensio return slices of the
      input (via_nomen ignores its piscina argument), and
      via_directorium returns a slice too, except that its answers "."
      and "/" are fresh copies in the piscina. If the input's bytes
      are freed or rolled back, the slices go with them. via_iungere
      and via_normalizare copy into the piscina (except that
      via_normalizare hands an empty input straight back). Copy with
      chorda_transcribere when you need a slice to outlive the
      input.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a relative path into an absolute one?
    <responsum symbolum="via_absoluta via_normalizare via_directorium_currens via_est_absoluta">via_absoluta
      joins the current directory (via_directorium_currens, getcwd)
      with the path and then calls via_normalizare; an input that is
      already absolute is only normalized. It is purely lexical:
      symlinks are not resolved and the path does not have to exist.
      If getcwd fails the input comes back unchanged, still relative
      and not normalized, with no error signal, so test
      via_est_absoluta on the result when it matters. Caution (audit
      2026-10-18, unfixed): getcwd also "fails" for a valid current
      directory longer than 4096 bytes (fixed stack buffer, ERANGE, no
      retry), so via_directorium_currens returns the empty chorda and
      via_absoluta("sub/../file") returns the relative input
      unchanged.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can via_normalizare handle relative paths that climb more than one level with ../?
    <responsum symbolum="via_normalizare">No,
      not correctly. It resolves '.', '//' and 'x/..' and keeps a
      leading ".." on a relative path, but a ".." that meets a kept
      ".." on its stack pops it instead of being kept: "../../foo"
      comes out as "foo", "../.." as "." and "a/../../.." as ".".
      A single "../foo" and absolute paths are fine ("/.." is "/").
      Do not normalize relative paths that climb more than one level
      until this is fixed.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do via_nomen and via_directorium return for trailing slashes and the root?
    <responsum symbolum="via_nomen via_directorium">They
      follow POSIX basename and dirname for trailing slashes:
      "/foo/bar/" gives "bar" and "/foo". For the root "/", via_nomen
      returns the empty chorda (POSIX basename returns "/", changed on
      purpose: the root has no name) and via_directorium returns "/".
      A name with no slash has directory ".", and so does the empty
      path. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed): on an input of 2^31
      bytes or more the s32 scan index overflows and no slash is
      found, so for "/dir/" + 2^31 bytes + "/name.txt" via_nomen
      returns the whole input, via_directorium returns "." and
      via_extensio returns "".</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what counts as the extension of a file name, and what about dotfiles?
    <responsum symbolum="via_extensio via_nomen_radix">via_extensio
      returns the last dot and everything after it, dot included
      ("archive.tar.gz" gives ".gz"). A name starting with a dot has no
      extension at all, even with a second dot: ".bashrc" and
      ".config.json" both give "", and via_nomen_radix returns the
      whole name. Only the last path component is looked at, so a dot
      in a directory name never counts.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check whether a path exists, and should I use via_existit or filum_existit?
    <responsum symbolum="via_existit filum_existit lectiones_notare">via_existit
      takes a chorda and uses stat, so it is VERUM for directories and
      for files you cannot read. filum_existit takes a C string and
      tries fopen(via, "r"), so it is FALSUM for a file you cannot
      read (on macOS a readable directory opens, so it is VERUM there
      too). Both record the answer (X or A) in the fabrica read
      ledger. via_existit returns FALSUM for an empty path or one of
      IV * MXXIV bytes or more. Caution (audit 2026-10-18, unfixed):
      via_existit ignores the chorda length past an embedded NUL byte,
      because stat sees only the prefix, so "/\0nusquam_zzz" or
      "/etc/hosts\0/nope" gives VERUM instead of FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does via_existit write to a ledger file, and what does it cost?
    <responsum symbolum="via_existit lectiones_notare" via="lib/fabrica.worklog.md">The
      build layer (fabrica) records what a generator looked up so it
      can decide when to rebuild. aedilis's include resolution goes
      through via_existit, and a header it looked for and did not find
      is a negative dependency: creating it later would change the
      result. lectiones_notare does nothing unless the environment
      variable FABRICA_LECTIONES names a file, so ordinary programs pay
      one getenv per call on top of the stat.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
